<p class="kicker">Philosophy · What we stand for</p>

# Ideology

<p class="lead">Why <strong>llama.cpp</strong>, <strong>Llama UI</strong> and the apps exist. <strong>Product</strong> covers how they fit together, <strong>Design</strong> how they look.</p>

## Personal and private

The model runs on a device you already own: your phone, laptop or desktop. It is yours to choose, set up and keep, and it runs the way you configured it.

Your conversations stay on that device, and everything works offline, without an account. There is nothing to sign in to and nothing to send, so what you ask stays private and secure by default. That is what personal AI means here.

<Figure n={1} caption="One device holds the conversations, the server and the model. The model is downloaded from Hugging Face once; nothing else leaves.">
	<Machine />
</Figure>

## Open to everyone

Open models run on hardware people already have, from a phone to a server. Making them run well there means capable AI doesn't depend on a subscription, a fast connection or a data centre.

Access to AI should be as open as access to information: available to anyone, wherever they live and whatever they can pay. Running it well on ordinary machines is how llama.cpp works towards that.

<Figure n={2} caption="Common machines and roughly the largest model each runs at a 4-bit quant. The bar is the model's share of the memory.">
	<Reach />
</Figure>

## Simple by default

Running a model used to take a terminal and terms like GGUF, quantization and speculative decoding. Most people want help with a letter, a photo or a question, and need none of that.

Nothing comes pre-installed. The Llama app suggests models that fit your device and recommends one above the rest: small enough for any device, and able to read, see and hear. You choose; the app downloads your choice from Hugging Face and keeps the server running.

Managing models is the native apps' job, so people rarely have to think about it. In Llama Chat, the model manager and Discover are for power users and should stay off until someone turns them on; anyone else should find a model by name in the global search, next to their conversations and projects.

Sampling, penalties, system prompts, custom recipes and every other setting stay available, one step away rather than on the first screen. Where exactly to draw that line is still open.

<Figure n={3} caption="The first run with Llama for Mac, in four frames from the real app and Llama Chat. Nothing comes pre-installed; settings wait until someone asks.">
	<Storyboard />
</Figure>

## Shared

A server doesn't have to serve one person. One machine can serve a family, a team, a lab or a neighbourhood, so people without the hardware can use it too.

<Figure n={4} caption="One server, run by one person, used by others on their own devices.">
	<Sharing />
</Figure>

## Three layers

Between a person and the AI are three layers: the **application** (the UI and UX), **inference** (the engine and server that run the model) and the **model** (the weights). The **harness**, the agentic loop and the tools it can call, bridges the first two: the loop runs in the app, and its tools run in the app or in llama-server.

The weights are what everything else is built on, so they stay open. In the Llama stack, every layer above them can run on your own hardware, with no provider in between.

<Figure n={5} caption="The three layers, from the one people use down to the weights, with the Llama part on each.">
	<Layers />
</Figure>

## Voice

We say what the product does, with real numbers, and show the real product. No hype, no fear, no superlatives.

<Figure n={6} caption="What we write, and what we don't.">
	<Voice />
</Figure>

## In practice

- **Product:** a suggested model and sensible defaults, with advanced settings one step away. It always shows which model runs on which server, needs no account and works offline.
- **Design:** neutral, so the conversation stands out, and open, so anyone can restyle it.
- **Communications:** the real app, real figures and the same plain voice.

<Figure n={7} caption="A model row as the app shows it, the greys with one accent, and llama.app's link preview.">
	<Shows />
</Figure>

<p class="colophon">Set in Llama Sans and Llama Mono.</p>
